# Target C: Woodall leaf saturation behind the square-root diameter braid

**Date:** 2026-10-02  
**Remote main rechecked before writing:** \`3bad7c12bf5133665be466d0396362108fadef2c\`  
**Parent research tip:** \`dea53ff0f6715efe70812b84fdaa5ad8de49e5bd\`  
**Status:** proof-only candidate. Level I and C-II remain open.

## 1. Purpose and scope

The current-main cubic star budget, together with canonical ZF, yields the
square-root lower bound for the matching number of the full actual diameter graph
proved in the parent research note:

\[
\nu(H)\ge
\left({1\over2\sqrt{12\pi\sqrt3}}-o(1)\right)\sqrt D.
\tag{1}
\]

That theorem gives a large ordered crossing braid, but does not control KKT mass.
The present note attacks the complementary hard case: suppose the actual diameter
matching is still sublinear.

Canonical C65 allows the lexicographic C-II source to be equipped with a
relative-interior normalized KKT stress for which every actual diameter edge is
positive. Hence the full actual diameter graph is the same loaded graph to which
canonical C-WOODALL applies.

The new point is that Woodall topology makes sublinear matching much more rigid than
an arbitrary sparse graph: almost every required diameter edge must be pendant.
This produces an exact graph/stress fork at distinct loaded terminals.

No new external theorem is imported.

## 2. A matching controls every non-pendant diameter edge

Let

\[
H=(B,E_H),\qquad h=|E_H|,\qquad \nu=\nu(H),
\]

be the full actual diameter graph. Let

\[
B_1:=\{b\in B:\deg_H(b)=1\}
\]

be the set of diameter leaves and put \(\ell=|B_1|\).

Call an edge **core** if neither endpoint lies in \(B_1\). Let \(E_{\rm core}\)
be the core-edge set.

### Theorem 2.1 -- core edge bound

\[
\boxed{|E_{\rm core}|\le2\nu+1.}
\tag{2}
\]

Consequently

\[
\boxed{
\ell\ge h-2\nu-1.
}
\tag{3}
\]

For the canonical source, ZF gives \(h\ge\lceil D\rceil\), so

\[
\boxed{
\ell\ge\lceil D\rceil-2\nu-1.
}
\tag{4}
\]

### Proof

Apply canonical C-WOODALL.

#### Forest case

Every component of \(H\) is a caterpillar. After deleting all degree-one vertices,
the remaining graph of one component is a path, possibly empty or a single vertex.
Therefore the core-edge graph is a disjoint union of paths.

A path with \(r\) edges has a matching of size \(\lceil r/2\rceil\ge r/2\).
Taking maximum matchings independently on the disjoint core paths gives a matching
of \(H\), so

\[
\nu\ge{|E_{\rm core}|\over2}.
\]

Thus \(|E_{\rm core}|\le2\nu\).

#### Cyclic case

C-WOODALL says \(H\) is one odd cycle with pendant leaves attached directly to
cycle vertices. The core edges are exactly the cycle edges. If its length is \(r\),
then

\[
\nu\ge {r-1\over2},
\]

hence \(r\le2\nu+1\).

This proves (2) in both cases.

Every edge outside \(E_{\rm core}\) is incident to a degree-one vertex. Choose one
degree-one endpoint from each such edge. These chosen vertices are distinct because
a degree-one vertex is incident to only one diameter edge. Hence

\[
\ell\ge |E_H\setminus E_{\rm core}|
=h-|E_{\rm core}|,
\]

which together with (2) proves (3). ZF gives (4). QED.

## 3. The hard matching regime is diameter-leaf saturated

Equation (4) gives an exact parameterized dichotomy.

For every \(\epsilon>0\),

\[
\nu\le\epsilon D
\quad\Longrightarrow\quad
\boxed{
\ell\ge(1-2\epsilon)D-O(1).
}
\tag{5}
\]

In particular, along any unbounded canonical sequence,

\[
\boxed{
\nu=o(D)
\quad\Longrightarrow\quad
\ell\ge D-o(D).
}
\tag{6}
\]

Thus a survivor cannot have both a sublinear actual-diameter matching and only a
sublinear population of diameter leaves.

The opposite case is already geometrically strong. If \(\nu\ge\epsilon D\), a
maximum matching itself gives \(\epsilon D\) vertex-disjoint actual diameter chords.
They are pairwise properly crossing by the diameter-thrackle property, and the exact
cyclic-order lemma in the parent note makes them one offset matching on two alternating
hull shores.

Hence the global support fork is

\[
\boxed{
\text{linear crossing diameter braid}
\quad\text{or}\quad
D-o(D)\text{ diameter leaves}.
}
\tag{7}
\]

The current-main cubic incidence theorem prevents the second branch from hiding all
diameter support in \(O(1)\) hubs: (1) still forces \(\nu=\Omega(\sqrt D)\).


## 3A. The hard case has only square-root many nonleaf diameter carriers

Let

\[
B_{\ge2}:=\{b\in B:\deg_H(b)\ge2\},
\qquad n_{\ge2}:=|B_{\ge2}|.
\]

Woodall gives a second exact matching bound:

\[
\boxed{n_{\ge2}\le3\nu+1.}
\tag{7a}
\]

In the forest case, after deleting all diameter leaves, the nonleaf vertices in each
caterpillar form a path. If a component contributes \(r_j>0\) nonleaf vertices, it
contributes \(r_j-1\) core edges. The number of diameter components is at most
\(\nu\), because a matching can choose one edge from every component. Hence

\[
n_{\ge2}
\le |E_{\rm core}|+\#\{\text{components}\}
\le2\nu+\nu=3\nu.
\]

In the cyclic case, the nonleaf vertices are exactly the vertices of the unique odd
cycle, whose length is at most \(2\nu+1\). This proves (7a).

There is also a leaf-edge version that removes isolated matching components. A
diameter component isomorphic to \(K_2\) consumes one matching edge, so there are at
most \(\nu\) such components. Consequently at least

\[
\boxed{
h-3\nu-1
}
\tag{7b}
\]

diameter edges join a degree-one diameter leaf to a nonleaf diameter carrier.

Thus if \(\nu\le K\sqrt D\),

\[
\boxed{
n_{\ge2}=O(\sqrt D),
\qquad
\#\{\text{leaf edges attached to nonleaf carriers}\}
=D-O(\sqrt D).
}
\tag{7c}
\]

In particular some nonleaf diameter carrier is incident with at least

\[
\boxed{
{h-3\nu-1\over 3\nu+1}
}
\tag{7d}
\]

pendant diameter leaves. At the square-root scale this is \(\Omega(\sqrt D)\).
The cubic star budget prevents all \(D-O(\sqrt D)\) pendant incidences from
collapsing onto only \(O(1)\) such carriers, but Woodall now shows that the entire
hard branch lives on only square-root many spine/cycle carriers.


## 3B. Contact leaves are paid by outer-walk excess

Now use the canonical positive contact graph \(C\). Let

\[
L_{11}:=
\{b\in B:\deg_H(b)=1,\ \deg_C(b)=1\},
\qquad \ell_{11}:=|L_{11}|.
\]

Write \(b_{\rm out}\) for the outer-face boundary-walk length of \(C\), counted with
edge multiplicity, and retain the canonical notation

\[
x_B:=b_{\rm out}-q.
\]

### Lemma 3B.1 -- double-leaf charge

\[
\boxed{\ell_{11}\le x_B+1.}
\tag{7e}
\]

### Proof

Every vertex of \(B\) is a strict force-hull vertex and hence lies on the outer face.
A contact leaf \(p\in L_{11}\) is incident with one contact edge, which is a bridge
and occurs twice in the outer-face boundary walk. Two contact leaves cannot be
adjacent here: in a connected graph that would make the entire positive contact
component a single \(K_2\), whereas canonical C65 makes that component span Euclidean
diameter \(D>1\).

Delete all vertices of \(L_{11}\) and their incident contact edges. Their incident
edges are therefore distinct. Removing a pendant vertex from a connected plane graph
preserves connectivity of the remaining graph and decreases the outer-face walk
length by exactly two. Hence the remaining
outer-face walk has length

\[
b'=b_{\rm out}-2\ell_{11}.
\]

Every remaining loaded vertex is still an extreme point of the remaining geometric
vertex set, so it is still on the outer face. If \(q'=q-\ell_{11}\) loaded vertices
remain, then \(b'\ge q'-1\); the minus one only covers the degenerate possibility
that one loaded vertex remains after all deletions. Therefore

\[
b_{\rm out}-2\ell_{11}
\ge q-\ell_{11}-1,
\]

which is exactly (7e). QED.

### Lemma 3B.2 -- exact collinear lock at a double leaf

Let \(p\in L_{11}\), let \(l\) be its unique contact neighbor, and let \(a\) be its
unique diameter partner. If the corresponding KKT multipliers are \(\alpha,\beta>0\),
then

\[
\boxed{
p-l={p-a\over D},
\qquad
\alpha=D\beta,
\qquad
|l-a|=D-1.
}
\tag{7f}
\]

Indeed equilibrium at \(p\) is

\[
\alpha(p-l)=\beta(p-a).
\]

The two vectors have lengths one and \(D\), so positivity forces the displayed
collinearity and scalar identity.

Thus contact leaves do not provide arbitrary local geometry: they are exact
one-unit inward locks on their unique diameter bars.

### Consequence at square-root matching scale

Assume again that

\[
\nu\le K\sqrt D.
\]

By (4),

\[
\ell\ge D-O(\sqrt D).
\]

If \(x_B=o(D)\), Lemma 3B.1 shows that only \(o(D)\) of these diameter leaves can
also be contact leaves. Therefore

\[
\boxed{
\#\{b:\deg_H(b)=1,\ \deg_C(b)\in\{2,3\}\}
=D-o(D).
}
\tag{7g}
\]

The degree set \(\{2,3\}\) uses canonical C26: a loaded terminal has positive
contact degree at most three.

Every vertex counted in (7g) is incident with at least two unit contacts. Since one
contact edge has at most two such endpoints, the number of distinct contact edges
incident with this leaf population is at least the size of the population. Hence

\[
\boxed{
x_B=o(D),\ \nu=O(\sqrt D)
\Longrightarrow
D-o(D)\text{ distinct unit contacts touch diameter leaves.}
}
\tag{7h}
\]

More quantitatively, if \(x_B\le\lambda D\) and \(\nu\le K\sqrt D\), then at least

\[
(1-\lambda)D-O(\sqrt D)
\tag{7i}
\]

diameter leaves have contact degree two or three, and at least that many distinct
unit-contact edges touch them.

So the square-root hard case now has an outer-walk/contact fork before any multiplier
estimate is used:

\[
\boxed{
x_B=\Omega(D)
\quad\text{or}\quad
\text{a linear unit-contact collar is incident with diameter leaves}.
}
\tag{7j}
\]

The second alternative is a support statement, not a claim that those unit contacts
form one geometric chart or carry a uniform amount of KKT stress.

## 4. Exact KKT load split on the pendant edges

Fix the relative-interior normalized KKT stress

\[
\alpha_e>0,\qquad \beta_h>0,\qquad
\sum_{h\in E_H}\beta_h=1.
\tag{8}
\]

Put

\[
E_{\rm leaf}:=E_H\setminus E_{\rm core},
\qquad
\beta_{\rm leaf}:=\sum_{h\in E_{\rm leaf}}\beta_h.
\tag{9}
\]

For every \(h\in E_{\rm leaf}\), choose one degree-one endpoint \(b_h\).
The chosen endpoints are distinct. Since \(h=b_ha_h\) is the only diameter edge
incident with \(b_h\),

\[
g_{b_h}
:=\sum_k\beta_{b_hk}(p_{b_h}-p_k)
=\beta_h(p_{b_h}-p_{a_h}),
\]

and therefore

\[
\boxed{|g_{b_h}|=D\beta_h.}
\tag{10}
\]

KKT equilibrium at \(b_h\) gives

\[
\sum_{y:\,b_hy\in C}\alpha_{b_hy}(p_{b_h}-p_y)
=g_{b_h}.
\tag{11}
\]

All contact vectors in (11) have length one. Triangle inequality yields

\[
\sum_{y:\,b_hy\in C}\alpha_{b_hy}\ge D\beta_h.
\tag{12}
\]

Summing over the distinct chosen leaf endpoints,

\[
\boxed{
\sum_{h\in E_{\rm leaf}}
\sum_{y:\,b_hy\in C}\alpha_{b_hy}
\ge D\beta_{\rm leaf}.
}
\tag{13}
\]

A contact edge has at most two chosen endpoints. Therefore, if
\(C_{\rm leaf}\) denotes the set of contact edges incident with at least one chosen
diameter leaf,

\[
\boxed{
\sum_{e\in C_{\rm leaf}}\alpha_e
\ge {D\over2}\beta_{\rm leaf}.
}
\tag{14}
\]

This is an exact bridge from pendant diameter stress to actual unit-contact stress.
No lower bound on an individual multiplier is used.

## 5. If leaf stress is small, a core diameter becomes heavy

All nonleaf diameter stress is carried by \(E_{\rm core}\), whose size is at most
\(2\nu+1\). Hence for every \(0<\eta<1\),

\[
\beta_{\rm leaf}<\eta
\quad\Longrightarrow\quad
\boxed{
\max_{h\in E_{\rm core}}\beta_h
\ge {1-\eta\over2\nu+1}.
}
\tag{15}
\]

Taking \(\eta=1/2\), every canonical source satisfies one of

\[
\boxed{
\sum_{e\in C_{\rm leaf}}\alpha_e\ge {D\over4},
}
\tag{16}
\]

or

\[
\boxed{
\max_{h\in E_{\rm core}}\beta_h
\ge {1\over4\nu+2}.
}
\tag{17}
\]

Thus the hard support regime does not merely say that KKT weights might be tiny.
It localizes where that escape can occur.

If

\[
\nu\le K\sqrt D
\tag{18}
\]

for a fixed \(K\), then the second alternative gives

\[
\boxed{
\max_{h\in E_{\rm core}}\beta_h
\ge\left({1\over4K}-o(1)\right)D^{-1/2}.
}
\tag{19}
\]

This is parametrically larger than the \(D^{-1}\) average scale of a linear
diameter support.

## 6. A heavy core edge forces a large contact reaction

The current-main midpoint-energy theorem gives a source-wide diameter-star aperture
\(\varepsilon_D=O(D^{-1/3})=o(1)\). At any fixed diameter endpoint \(b\), all vectors

\[
p_b-p_k,\qquad bk\in E_H,
\]

therefore lie in one cone of aperture \(\varepsilon_D\).

Let

\[
s_b=\sum_k\beta_{bk}.
\]

Choosing the cone bisector as a unit vector \(n_b\),

\[
g_b\cdot n_b
\ge D s_b\cos(\varepsilon_D/2),
\]

so

\[
\boxed{
|g_b|\ge(1-o(1))D s_b.
}
\tag{20}
\]

If \(h=ab\) is the heavy core edge from (19), then \(s_a,s_b\ge\beta_h\).
Thus both endpoints satisfy

\[
|g_a|,|g_b|
\ge
\left({1\over4K}-o(1)\right)\sqrt D.
\tag{21}
\]

Canonical C26 gives contact degree at most three at a loaded terminal. Applying
(11) at \(a\) and \(b\), at least one unit contact incident with each endpoint has
multiplier at least one third of the resultant magnitude. Hence

\[
\boxed{
\text{the core-heavy branch contains a contact multiplier }
\alpha_e
\ge
\left({1\over12K}-o(1)\right)\sqrt D.
}
\tag{22}
\]

No contradiction is claimed from (22); it is a concrete stress concentration
target that is absent from a support-only matching theorem.

## 7. Combined square-root hard-case normal form

Combine the parent square-root braid theorem with the present result.

The absolute lower bound is

\[
\nu(H)\ge
\left({1\over2\sqrt{12\pi\sqrt3}}-o(1)\right)\sqrt D.
\tag{23}
\]

If a subsequence has \(\nu(H)=O(\sqrt D)\), then simultaneously

\[
\boxed{\ell=D-O(\sqrt D)}
\tag{24}
\]

and one of the following holds:

1. **leaf-loaded branch**
   \[
   \sum_{e\in C_{\rm leaf}}\alpha_e\ge D/4;
   \tag{25}
   \]

2. **core-concentrated branch**
   one core diameter has
   \[
   \beta_h=\Omega(D^{-1/2}),
   \tag{26}
   \]
   and some unit contact incident with each of its two endpoints has
   \[
   \alpha_e=\Omega(\sqrt D).
   \tag{27}
   \]

Meanwhile the matching itself is an \(\Omega(\sqrt D)\) exact two-shore crossing
braid whose midpoints lie in one \(O(D^{2/3})\) disk.

This is a materially narrower hard case than

\[
\text{large matching but arbitrary tiny diameter weights}.
\]

The support topology and normalized stress now have only two ways to coexist at the
square-root scale: linear aggregate unit-contact load at pendant diameter terminals,
or a super-average core diameter/contact stress concentration.

## 8. What remains open

Neither branch is yet contradictory.

- In the leaf-loaded branch, (14) is aggregate multiplier mass, not a geometric chart
  concentration theorem. The next useful step is to couple the \(D-O(\sqrt D)\)
  pendant diameter terminals and their actual unit contacts to the current ball-hull
  / loaded-leaf atlas or to the full-core outer-walk currency.
- In the core-concentrated branch, (22) is a large local contact stress but current
  theory has no upper bound excluding such a multiplier. A closing argument should
  propagate it through the common 2-edge-connected contact hub or turn it into a
  finite source-preserving release.
- If \(\nu\) is larger than \(O(\sqrt D)\), the exact crossing braid itself grows
  beyond the cubic hard scale and should be attacked directly by the two-shore
  contact/outer-walk geometry.

The macroscopic inactive-container branch remains independent.

**Disposition:** proof-only structural/stress reduction. C-II remains open. No
computation, numerical experiment, verifier execution, or CI output is used.
